Predictive modeling is centered around making informed predictions with the available data. However, the most well-known model, linear regression, is limited in its ability to generalize, particularly when it comes to handling complex data points. To address this issue, we will delve into the properties of linear regression and related concepts to grasp the full potential of this fundamental statistical tool. Specifically, we will examine the impact of regularization on model performance, understanding that this technique is not merely a method to mitigate the effects of overfitting, but rather a way to effectively control the model's capabilities. Through theoretical analysis, simulation studies, and the application of Bayesian inference, we will uncover new insights into linear regression and related methods. These findings will be further reinforced by the application of machine learning and deep learning techniques. Our ultimate goal is to provide a more in-depth understanding of linear regression and its place within the realm of predictive modeling. To support this endeavor, we will develop and provide R code for replicating our experiments. Users can install the necessary packages, including "rstanarm" and "bplm," using the command install.packages("rstanarm") and install.packages("bplm", recogize = TRUE), respectively. After loading these packages, the code can be executed by running bplmRstanR( ) and stanar1i( ) in separate R sessions. Additionally, we will provide a Shiny app that allows users to interactively explore the impact of regularization on model parameters. This app can be launched by running the following command: shiny::shinyUI(
